      {
        "review_id": 662624612,
        "review_link": "https://www.tripadvisor.com/ShowUserReviews-g293890-d310712-r662624612-Pashupatinath_Temple-Kathmandu_Kathmandu_Valley_Bagmati_Zone_Central_Region.html",
        "title": "A place full of highly intense experiences",
        "text": "A functioning temple of high esteem for Hindus. hence extremely crowded. also, if you are not an asian hindu you will not be allowed to enter the main temple precinct. \n\nthat said the temple has 4 parts - the main temple precinct which houses the actual deity and the nandi (holy bull), the secondary precinct which is outside the main precinct and has subsidiary temples and all sorts of sadhus waiting to make a money from foreigners who want to click a photo, the burning ghat or crematorium and lastly the other side of the river on top of the hill.\n\nlets start with the last first. the \"river\" is actually almost a drain (sorry for this but this is what the Government has transformed the Bagmati river to in this area). it is a very narrow strip of water that hardly flows and is full of rubbish. on the opposite side of this stream is a small hill lined with small temples. you should climb all the way up as there are beautiful views from there. also halfway down the stairs you can also sit down and see the process of burning of dead bodies (for whoever is mentally aligned to that). \n\nback on the temple side of the river / stream, you have the burning ghats - these are just ghats and normally people who come there to burn their departed family are present. \n\nbeyond this is the secondary precinct which has a lot of smaller temples - you can visit these even as a non-Hindu. some of the temple architecture is really fascinating and beautiful. this is a place of living tradition and culture and hence quite interesting and at the same time a place that deserves respect to be shown.\n\nfinally you have the main temple precinct that houses the main shrine and the huge giant nandi (bull) statue and also multitude of smaller temples. there is always a line / queue to see the main temple but it is quite organized and there is no hustling by the temple functionaries and no pressure for paying money and things like that. you can if you want, view the shivling (the main idol) and walk away without paying a single penny. the organization inside the main precinct while not top notch is pretty decent given the crowd that it handles. no photography or video or mobile phones are allowed in this place. i went in to the main temple with my dslr closed - one person asked me how you have such a big camera here - i told him it is switched off and closed and he was OK.  everywhere else it is not a problem. you have to enter without shoes of course. \n\nwe had a guided tour, where the guide made us open our shoes and keep all our leather belongings in a shop and then gave us rubber slippers to use till we came back. this is the normal procedure. you can keep your wallets and stuff also but depends on your risk appetite.   \n\nthere are huge crowds all the while and if visiting with small children then it is a must to keep good control over them as it is very easy for a child to get lost in the crowd. we went on a saturday around 10:30 to 11 in the morning. it was quite crowded but not in a way that you cannot move at all.\n\noverall this is a must visit to see religious fervour and some tradition and culture. we did not wait around for the evening arati  on the banks as the sight of the river was quite depressing.",
        "review_tip": null,
